// Module `float`, extended (LANG-7): library members of the core module `float` (SEM-2),
// over its core conversions (from_int, to_int, widen, narrow, to_bits32/64,
// from_bits32/64; core/core/float.hirsel).
//
// A library function cannot be generic over f32 and f64: no bound names the float types
// (OWN-4), so each operation is written at each width it serves, as Go's ParseFloat takes
// a bit size. An f32 result is not `float.narrow` of the f64 one: that rounds twice.
//
// Parsing (Rust core::num::dec2flt, Go strconv.ParseFloat). The decimal mantissa is read
// into a u64 (19 significant digits) and a decimal exponent.
//  1. Exact fast path (Clinger): mantissa below 2^53 (f32: 2^24) and |exponent| <= 22
//     (f32: 10): `float.from_int` of the mantissa, multiplied or divided by an exact power
//     of ten, which IEEE rounds once.
//  2. Eisel-Lemire: a 128-bit product of the mantissa and a truncated power of five,
//     assembled into the result's bits and made with `float.from_bits64` (`from_bits32`).
//     v1 has no u128 and no high-half multiply (Go bits.Mul64), so each 64 x 64 product is
//     four 32 x 32 partial products and their carries: about 4x the multiplies.
//     The 651 powers of five, two u64 words each, are a private function over one `match`
//     on the exponent: a `const` holds one plain value and there is no fixed-size array,
//     so the table cannot be a constant.
//  3. Slow path, when step 2 cannot decide the rounding (more than 19 significant digits
//     near a halfway point): a big decimal of at most 768 significant digits, shifted by
//     powers of two. Its digit buffer is a Bytes of capacity 768, admitted once from the
//     current limit; Rust keeps it in a fixed-size array on the stack, which v1 cannot
//     declare. Only this step allocates, so only it can return Capacity.

/// Why a float did not parse: the lexeme's error, or the slow path's buffer refused.
pub type Error = Parse(ParseError) | Capacity(Capacity)

/// Parses the whole of s as a decimal f64: an optional '-', digits with an optional
/// fraction, an optional exponent (the JSON number grammar plus a leading '+' in the
/// exponent; "inf", "nan", '+' before the number and hex are refused). Correctly rounded,
/// ties to even.
/// Fails: Error(Parse(Empty | Invalid(i))), i the byte position; Error(Parse(OutOfRange))
/// when the value rounds to an infinity (Go returns ErrRange there; Rust returns inf).
/// A value too small rounds to a signed zero and is Ok. Error(Capacity(c)) only from
/// step 3. Alloc: nothing, or 768 bytes in step 3, refunded before return.
/// Cost: O(len); step 3 is O(digits x shifts), at most about 768 x 30 digit steps.
/// Ex: `let x = float.parse64(token)?`
pub fn parse64(s: String) -> Result[f64, Error]

/// As parse64, rounded once to f32. Ex: `let y = float.parse32(token)?`
pub fn parse32(s: String) -> Result[f32, Error]

/// Whether x is neither infinite nor NaN: its exponent bits (`float.to_bits64`) are not
/// all ones. For an f32 use `float.is_finite(float.widen(x))`, which is exact.
/// Cost: O(1). Ex: `float.is_finite(x)`
pub fn is_finite(x: f64) -> bool
